    jl:hasAbstract "",
        "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:   notes    Glossary of abbreviations following text.      Added printers from colophon: Shelomoh b. Mosheh of Fürth, Koppel b. Shelomoh Sulzbach of Fürth, Matittyah b. Lob Gutman of Fürth, Yehudah b. Menahem of Roedelheim, Shelomoh b. Yoseph of Fürth-im-wald, and Gedaliah Dov b. Lipman of Zirendorff-Fürth.      Text layout of this edition: The center is the text of Joseph Karo’s Shulhan ’Arukh and Moses Isserles’ Mapah; the surrounding columns are the text of Turei Zahav Magen David by David ha-levi Segal on the gutter-side, and Magen Avraham by Abraham Abele Gombiner on the margin side. On the outer margins, Moses Rivkes’ Be’er ha-Golah is at the top, while Menahem-Mendel Aurbach’s ’Ateret Zekenim covers the center-lower margin. Interspersed with the margin commentaries for the sections covering the laws of Passover are the notes written by Jacob ben Joseph Reischer for his printed Ḥok Ya’akov, originally printed independently at Jessnitz 1719; this at times covers entire leaves, and when this occurs, the leaves are unnumbered.      Haskamot of this edition: Joseph b. Menahem Steinhardt of Fürth, signing in ’Niederrheim’, 1 Nisan 1765; Samuel Heillman in Metz, dated 19 Shevat 1763; Joshua-Heschel Levov of Schwäbisch-Ansbach, 10 Tamuz 1763; Judah-Emerüch of Schonach, signing in Fürth 10 Kislev 1763; Eli’ezer Katzenellenbogen of Haguenau, dated 1 Nisan 1763; Zvi-Hirsch Kahana of Wallerstein-Harburg, dated 22 Tevet 1762.      Printer information relays the address of the printer on the Alexanderstrasse in Fürth.",
        "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:  General Note  Steinschneider notes that this edition was printed in two volumes, with each volume containing two titles for each volume of the Shulhan Arukh.  Citation/Ref. Note    BenJakob, Otsar Hasefarim,   gimel 81.  Citation/Ref. Note    Friedberg, Beit Eked Sefarim,   gimel 183.  Citation/Ref. Note    Strauss, Berthold, Cat. of B. Strauss Library,   5587-5590.  General Note  Each volume has a separate title. The opening volume (Oraḥ Ḥaim and Yoreh De'ah) featured miniature woodcuts of some of the major rabbinic scholars post-Gaonic times (9th century): Rashi of Troyes, Maimonides, Jacob ha-levi Moellin, Moses Cordovero, Moses Isserles, and finally Gur-Aryeh ha-levi Finzi (wearing clerical vestments - a Ferraiolo with a Clerical collar). These illustrations were discontinued on the later three titles.  Citation/Ref. Note    Steinschneider, M. Cat. Libr. Hebr. Bodl.,   5940 : 20  General Note  This was an inaugural edition of the Shulḥan 'Arukh printed with the basic texts (Joseph Karo's Shulḥan 'Arukh and Moses Isserles' Mapah) with the commentary of Gur-Aryeh b. Benjamin ha-Leṿi Finzi of Mantua on the outer borders of the pages. The volume begins with an introductory paragraph by David b. 'Ezri'el Finzi, introductions by Gur-Aryeh Finzi (Casalmaggiore 18th century; grandson of the previously mentioned Gur-Aryeh Finzi). The third heading (titled אריה שאג, Aryeh Sha'ag) is a written eulogy for Gur-Aryeh Finzi (the elder) by his grandson.  General Note  Title-pages within ornamental borders.  General Note  Edited with an introduction by his grandson, Gur Aryeh.",
        "Does the BHB think Hanau is Hanover? Check the link above! Chaim Meiselman adds: The Christian printer was likely using the Latin name for Hanau - Hanoviae - and it gives off the impression to contemporary viewers with Eastern European Yiddish in mind that it is Hanover.",
        "The continuing title statement adds that for the Yoreh De'ah volume of this edition of the Shulhan Arukh, the commentary of Even ha-'Ozer (by Ozer b. Meir of Klimontów) was 'like the Props printers of Amsterdam', i.e. the 1742-3 first edition of this commentary.Text layout for this edition: the text (Joseph Karo's Shulhan Arukh, Moses Isseles' Mapah) is at the center text-block; below is Judah Ashkenazi's Be'er Hetiv, and below that (with headed title) is Abraham Openhaim's Eshel Avraham; at the upper margins is Moses Rivkes' Be'er ha-Golah." ;
