<http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/2bff39ba-ee0a-518a-9f83-aa441a5cffcb> a gndo:Work ;
    gndo:associatedPlace "Sulzbach am Main" ;
    gndo:author "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/dd4fc288-3adb-5c98-865f-29deaf144cfd" ;
    gndo:dateOfPublication "1741" ;
    gndo:formerOwner "Aaron Wise",
        "Baltimore Hebrew College",
        "Barukh (Barukh Hayyim) Almanzi",
        "Columbia University Libraries",
        "Giuseppe Almanzi",
        "Gottschalk Scheftel",
        "Joseph Arwas",
        "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library",
        "Samuel A. Neuhausen",
        "Stephen Samuel Wise",
        "Temple Emanu-el",
        """יוסף צבי הרש ווייס
 Joseph Tsvi Hirsch Weiss""" ;
    gndo:language "Hebrew" ;
    gndo:owner "Columbia University Libraries",
        "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library" ;
    jl:describedAt "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000109136&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0" ;
    jl:hasAbstract "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:  notes    The author is identified as Yosef b. Eliezer Ettinger of Hölleschau (today Holešov near Zlin in the east-central Czech Republic) who served as an Av-Beth-Din in Kraków at the time of printing. Elements of writing are taken from the author’s relatives, also rabbis; columns in the book have titles such as ’by the [author’s] brother, Yehuda-Loeb ha-levi of Lvov’ and author’s brother-in-law Shelomoh of the Lichtenstadt Rabbinate (today Hroznětín in the north Czech Republic); the father-in-law of the author’s brother, Eisek Prager of the Glogau Rabbinate.      On l. 2, a paragraph is attributed to the eminent rabbi of Hölleschau, Shabbatai ben Meir HaKohen (1621-1662), also known as the Shach, because he authored the commentary Sifte Kohen.      Haskamah of the author’s brother, Yehudah-Loeb ha-levi of Lvov; introduction by the author’s father.      Title page is illustrated heavily around an architechtural style; miniature woodcuts are built-in to the scheme. At the top, Jacob dreams of angels ascending a ladder; Abraham greets Melchizedek in Salem; Moses accepts the Tablets of the Law at Sinai. Abraham and Isaac flank the sides; at the bottom corners, Elijah appears as an angelic figure and blows a ram’s horn with the Messiah, and the Hebrews sacrifice the Passover Lamb in Egypt.   references    Steinschneider, M. Cat. Libr. Hebr. Bodl., no. 5911 : 1." ;
    dc:publisher "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/2ead7558-15f3-5ab8-9446-0418a8106b60" ;
    dc:title "ʻEdut be-Yosef" ;
    dcterms:created "2024-03-17T14:49:37.738985"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:prefLabel "ʻEdut be-Yosef" .

