    jl:hasAbstract "",
        "<p>&nbsp;ספר עין ישראל עם פירוש מספיק מלוקט</p>",
        "<p>Imprint unknown</p>",
        "According to the National Library of Israel (BHB), this edition of  Kotnot 'Or/'En Ya'akov is actually the first volume of a 2-volume work. The second volume was also printed in Amsterdam in 1684, but by a different printer, David de Castro Tartes.",
        "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:  Printed in double columns; the layout is in paragraph form with a heading catchword of the source of text. For Samuel Edels commentary, the paragraph begins Ma-Harsh.a. (מהרשא); for Yoshiahu Pinto’s commentary the paragraph begins Ha-Rif (הריף); verses are sourced at the outer margins.      Title pages of both parts have ornamental borders. Last p. of vol. 1 has woodcut decoration.      Haskamot of Joseph Steinhardt of Fürth, dated 10 Tevet 1764; Joshua-Hoeshel Levov of Ansbach and Schwabach, signing 7 Nisan 1764; Nathaniel Weill of Kahrlsruhe, with the Duchies of Baden and Durlach, signing 12 Shevat 1762. Afterword, an introduction by the printer and publisher, after which follows introductions written by the commentators on the volume - Samuel Edels, Yoshiahu Pinto, and finally Jacob ibn Habib.      Text ends with words of praise by the compiler’s son, Levi ibn Habib.",
        "Friedberg, Beit Eked Sefarim,'ayin 491 : 31  Haskamot of Meshullam Solomon Kohn of Furth (1739-1819), David Düssbak of Fürth. Both were ardent adherents to Jonathan Eyebeschütz of Altona.",
        "The title page does not include an explicit date of publication. However, according to the National Library of Israel, this edition was published sometime between 1587 and 1590 (based on other information that appears on the title page). The dates given in the JTS catalog are 1587-1591." ;
