<http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/5029120b-c55c-5acd-8ef3-fb2a82f484bd> a gndo:Work ;
    gndo:associatedPlace "İzmir" ;
    gndo:author "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/d34b22c1-992c-57ec-9c11-0a7f4e9f9ed7" ;
    gndo:dateOfPublication "1762" ;
    gndo:formerOwner "Russian State Library",
        "University of Pennsylvania Libraries",
        "Yosef Yitzhak Schneerson (Sixth Lubavicher Rebbe)",
        "Zekhariah Yosef Ḳabali" ;
    gndo:language "Hebrew" ;
    gndo:owner "Russian State Library",
        "University of Pennsylvania Libraries" ;
    gndo:printer "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/60441853-ab37-54c1-a1b2-4ed4d72ca974" ;
    jl:describedAt "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000123195&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0" ;
    jl:hasAbstract "",
        "The title is within an architectural design. Extensive floral pattern on columns, crowns on the upper portions, and lions flanking the lower mantelpiece. The title refers the reigning monarch, Mustafa III, Sultan of the Ottoman Empire (1717 - 1773). The author's introduction is on leaf [2] and is printed in a poetic form. Printed in double columns in small type. There are two leaves of indexes at the end of the volume. The indexes follow the order of the tractates of the Talmud and then Maimonides, and then listing titles of responsa. Added typesetters from the colophon: Mosheh G'iprus, Avraham Mosheh (?), Yaʻaḳov Yaʼavets, Elʻazar G'iprus, and their overseer Yitsḥaḳ ha-kohen. Errata and additions to the volume were printed at the end of the author's volume of responsa (Agurah be-oholekha, Thessaloniki: Refaʼel Yaʼuda Ḳelʻei and Mordekhai Naḥman, 1780-1781. Signatures: [1] - [58]² [59]²(-[59]2). Signature marks numbered on the lower margin of each page. First printing. Vinograd, |c Izmir 82 Bibliography of the Hebrew Book, |c 000123195" ;
    dc:title "Toʻafot reʼem" ;
    dcterms:created "2024-03-18T01:41:16.373258"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:prefLabel "Toʻafot reʼem" .

