<http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/56fa6afb-17f1-522d-8d48-28a7bbfb2ae2> a gndo:Work ;
    gndo:associatedPlace "Francofurti ad Moenum",
        "Frankfurt am Main",
        "Frankfurt to Main",
        "Lviv" ;
    gndo:author "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/7473840a-128a-538e-bccb-875de7a40c7f",
        "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/c5221993-e50e-5078-94db-453dad64a0c3" ;
    gndo:dateOfPublication "1752",
        "1756",
        "1815" ;
    gndo:formerOwner "Baltimore Hebrew College",
        "Gavriel ben Yakov Rosenberg",
        "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library",
        "Russian State Library",
        "Yosef Yitzhak Schneerson (Sixth Lubavicher Rebbe)" ;
    gndo:language "Hebrew" ;
    gndo:owner "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library",
        "Russian State Library" ;
    gndo:printer "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/e5dd78e3-1f35-5ee7-8309-2891deabb365" ;
    jl:describedAt "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000136248&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://search.worldcat.org/de/title/750667530",
        "https://search.worldcat.org/de/title/844712081",
        "https://search.worldcat.org/de/title/874901766" ;
    jl:hasAbstract "",
        "Why is this missing from BHB?? And Steinschneider.   Notes: Although in the historical timeline of Pilpul, the style of Talmudic analysis begun during the 16th century in Poland, this work is late - it probably is one of the most famous Pilpul works ever written.  Decorated title, and then detailed title after. Title statement from the first title: ...יעקב יושע ששימש בכתר הרבנות בקק לבוב והגליל ובקק ברלין וקק מיץ ובקק פפ דמיין : ויהא רעווא דמשמיא יסכימו על ידו להוציא מחשבתו אל הפועל אשר נשאו לבו לשבת בארץ הקדושה איה. Translation: ... Jacob Joshua, who served the 'crown of the Rabbinate' in the holy communities of L'vov, and surrounding ... and in Berlin, and in Metz, and in Frankfurt on the Main ; may it be that in the generosity of heaven, They will accede to his will to complete his desire to material, that which (he) carries in his heart ... to settle in the Holy Land, Amen, may it be His will (so).  Falk, the author, unfortunately, saw a very different ending - he died penniless in Frankfurt, 1756, no longer with a Rabbinate, in the aftermath of the Emden-Eyebeshütz affair; he is buried there.   ʻAl masekhtot Berakhot, Shabat, Pesaḥim, Betsah, Rosh ha-shanah, Sukah, Megilah u-ḳetsat ḥidushim mi-masekhet Yoma.  Friedberg, Beit Eked Sefarim,pei 418:(1). Ben-Jakob, Otsar ha-Sefarim,922:(1).  --Chaim Meiselman" ;
    dc:title "Pene Yehoshua" ;
    dcterms:created "2024-03-17T20:28:58.612920"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pene Yehoshua" .

