        "Yosef Yitzhak Schneerson (Sixth Lubavicher Rebbe)" ;
    gndo:language "Hebrew" ;
    gndo:owner "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library",
        "Russian State Library",
        "Washington University at St. Louis" ;
    gndo:printer "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/a736eba9-115f-5a26-8b03-4783c79c1125",
        "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/e7978d2e-0232-5f01-80e5-9b39e96d1d68" ;
    jl:describedAt "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000126708&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000126716&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,001790948&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,021316520&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,‬   000126716&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://search.worldcat.org/de/title/122652898" ;
    jl:hasAbstract "",
        "Notes by Chaim Meiselman :   Zedner, Cat. British Museum Library,p.283.BenJakob, Otsar ha-Sefarim,91 : (3).Ch.D. Friedberg, Beit Eked Sefarim,samekh 67 : 3.  Title note describes the lineage of the author. מהורר יחיאל... שמו נודע למשגב ... בקק מינסק בליטא ... בן שלמה אבד בשקלאב ... נכד ... מהרשל נין ונכד מאור הגולה רשי זל ... היוצא מהתנא ר. יוחנן הסנדלר ... דור רביעי לרבן גמליאל הזקן | . Translation: the author, his name is known and ascendant ... as Av-Beth-Din of Minsk, in 'Lita' (reference to the Lithuanian Diaspora), son of Shelomoh, Av-Beth-Din in Shklovv (Shklou, Belarus); grandchild to Mahar.sha.l. (Solomon b. Jehiel Luria, Lublin-Poland, 1510-1573); descendant of The Light of the Exile, Rashi (Solomon b. Isaac, Troyes-France, 11th century); he descended from ... Jochanan ha-Sandlar (2nd century Judea - Alexandria); descendant of four generations (i.e. great-grandson) to Gamaliel the Elder (leader of the late Sanhedrin, Judea, 1st century). The implication is the lineage of King David (Davidic-Solomonic royal lineage) was counted historically through the aforequoted figures, thus preserving it through eras of Jewish exile. This quote is from the author written to complete the title-author statement of the first edition (Kahrlsruhe, 1769) - however it is missing from the contents of the book, entries Rashi, Gamaliel, and Jochanan ha-Sandlar, add to that the lengthy introduction.  This work is not the first one of Rabbinic history and historiography, however, it differs from earlier as it follows the Rabbinic pilpul with regard to dates, personas, and historical periods. His sources are from the Talmud, Bible and some of the Apocrypha, and statement of the medieval Tosafists and Maimonides; Sefer ha-Kabalah by Abraham ibn Daud (12th cent.), Yuhasin by Abraham Zacuto, Sifte Yeshenim by Sabbetai Bas, and similiar Rabbinic-historical works. Examples of questions: Who was the Mishnaic Tyrannus-Rufus (introd.); What were the true historical personas of the Sabora'im (5th - 8th cents. Iraq); resolving Alexander the Great in the Mishnaic and Talmudic sources; The 10th century rediscorvery of the ten lost tribes by Eldad ha-Dani (second count, ls. 34-36); Gamaliel's exile with the Sanhedrin from Jerusalem to Ussa to Yabne (third count, ls. 18-19), and many other historical topics surrounding Judaic-Rabbinic history.  Title with just text and no ornamentation; block-letters be-otiyot Amsterdam - 'with the Amsterdam type. The opening word of the text is אדם, Adam, which is highlighted and decorated by a woodcut border. In this design, woodcut type was mistakenly left in, adding incongruent designs to the illustration. Lettered U..Introduction, and then a running appendix at the opening. Some appendices end the volume as well.Chronogram: בשנת ק.ו.רא. ה.ד.ו.רו.ת. מ.ראש (1808).",
        "Notes by Chaim Meiselman.   notes    This is the first printing of the historical-geneological volume Seder ha-Dorot by Jehiel Heilprin of Minsk. The author had died approximately 1746, and the work is posthumous. The title statement describes the printing of the volume by the generosity of a grandson of the author, Arye-Löb b. Eisek of Koidanov (today Dzyarzhynsk, Belarus), who wrote a lengthy introduction.      Title note describes the lineage of the author. מהורר יחיאל... שמו נודע למשגב ... בקק מינסק בליטא ... בן שלמה אבד בשקלאב ... נכד ... מהרשל נין ונכד מאור הגולה רשי זל ... היוצא מהתנא ר. יוחנן הסנדלר ... דור רביעי לרבן גמליאל הזקן | . Translation: the author, his name is known and ascendant ... as Av-Beth-Din of Minsk, in ’Lita’ (reference to the Lithuanian Diaspora), son of Shelomoh, Av-Beth-Din in Shklovv (Shklou, Belarus); grandchild to Mahar.sha.l. (Solomon b. Jehiel Luria, Lublin-Poland, 1510-1573); descendant of The Light of the Exile, Rashi (Solomon b. Isaac, Troyes-France, 11th century); he descended from ... Jochanan ha-Sandlar (2nd century Judea - Alexandria); descendant of four generations (i.e. great-grandson) to Gamaliel the Elder (leader of the late Sanhedrin, Judea, 1st century). The implication is the lineage of King David (Davidic-Solomonic royal lineage) was counted historically through the aforequoted figures, thus preserving it through eras of Jewish exile. This quote is from the author written to complete the title-author statement but it is missing from the contents of the book, entries Rashi, Gamaliel, and Joḥanan ha-Sandlar, as well as in the lengthy introduction.      This work is not the first one of Rabbinic history and historiography, however, it differs from earlier as it follows the Rabbinic pilpul with regard to dates, personas, and historical periods. His sources are from the Talmud, Bible and some of the Apocrypha, and statements of the medieval Tosafists and Maimonides; Sefer ha-Kabalah by Abraham ibn Daud (12th cent.), Yuhasin by Abraham Zacuto, Sifte Yeshenim by Sabbetai Bas, Tsemah-David of David Gunz, and similiar Rabbinic-historical works. Examples of questions: Who was the Mishnaic Tyrannus-Rufus (introd.); What were the true historical personas of the Sabora’im (5th - 8th cents. Iraq); resolving Alexander the Great in the Mishnaic and Talmudic sources; The 10th century rediscovery of the ten lost tribes by Eldad ha-Dani (second count, ls. 34-36); Gamaliel’s exile with the Sanhedrin from Jerusalem to Ussa to Yabne (third count, ls. 18-19), and many other historical topics surrounding Judaic-Rabbinic history.      Chronogram: ’ט’וב לי ’ת’ורת ’פ’יך ’מ’אלפי זהב וכסף לפק. With a dedication to Karl Friedrich, i.e. Charles Frederick, Grand Duke of Baden (referred to as Margrave of Baden-Durlach), 1728-1811.      Title features fine ornamentation surrounding ordered text block. Ornamentation throughout; angelic figures grace the opening words of the text, illustrating the opening topic of the creation of Adam and Eve in the Garden of Eden.      Haskamot: Nathaniel Weill of Kahrlsruhe and the general area of Baden, 1 Adar II 1769; Saul [Lowenstamm] of the Ashkenazic Community in Amsterdam, 7 Elul 1767; Abraham [Abish, originally from Lesno] of Frankfurt-am-Main, 29 Tevet 1765; Isaac ha-levi Horovitz of Altona-Hamburg-Wandsbek, 8 Tamuz 1766; of three av-beth-dins of Berlin, Joel b. Jekuthiel of Glogau, Gershon Unger, and Zanvyl Neugass, signing 28 Iyyar 1766; Joseph Steinhardt of Fürth, 11 Kislev 1769; Lemele ha-levi of greater Glogau, 4 Kislev 1765; Naftali-Hirsch Katzenellenbogen of Mannheim-Pfalz, 12 Tamuz 1768; Solomon of Chelmno, Elul 1765; Feivel b. Samuel Hellman of Leszno, 15 Heshvan 1765; Sha’ul ha-levi of Apta, 10 Elul 1765; Joseph ha-levi of Khmilnyk, 19 Elul 1765; Joshua-Heshel ha-kohen of Szczebrzeszyn, 9 Elul 1765; Abraham Katzenellenbogen of Brisk (Brest-Litovsk, Belarus), 14 Av 1765; Aryeh-Leib ha-levi Epstein of Koenigsburg, 27 Sivan 1765; Joel of Orłowo, 16 Tamuz 1765.   references    Steinschneider, M. Cat. Libr. Bodl., no. 5657 : 1.   notes    Includes bibliographical references.   contents    Seder yemot ʻolam -- Seder tanaʼim ṿe-amoraʼim -- Seder Meḥabrim u-sefarim.      ‫ סדר ימות עולם ־־ סדר תנאים ואמוראים -- סדר מחברים וספרים. ‬" ;
    dc:title "Seder ha-Dorot" ;
    dcterms:created "2024-03-17T21:00:11.469421"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seder ha-Dorot" .

