        "נפתלי בר משה נחשון שמואל" ;
    gndo:language "Hebrew" ;
    gndo:owner "Columbia University Libraries",
        "Comunita Ebraica di Venezia",
        "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library",
        "Russian State Library",
        "The Library of the Katz Center for Advanced Judaic Studies" ;
    gndo:printer "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/574b8e24-0e32-57e7-9cc6-70884d09a767",
        "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/98960633-0f12-5fdd-a3ac-cf3dd0a8bc37" ;
    jl:describedAt "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000180135&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000180159&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000180178&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000200802&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,063663853&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://search.worldcat.org/de/title/233117568" ;
    jl:hasAbstract "",
        "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:   General Note  Illustrated title; architechtural scheme imposed over the center text, with large depictions of (classical) Moses and (semitic) Aaron on each side-column.  General Note  Layout of the text for this edition: The center paragraph contains Joseph Karo's Shulhan Arukh with Moses Isserles' Mapah; the gutter-edge margin is composed of Moses Lima's Ḥelḳat Meḥoḳeḳ, and the outer margin is that of Samuel b. Uri Phoebus' Beit Shemu'el.  General Note  On leaves 142b - 148b there is a break in the text and (as an excursus) features Samuel b. Uri Phoebus' Ḳunṭros Shemot ha-Anashim u-Bet Shemot ha-Nashim ('miniature-tract regarding the names of men, and [also] the names of women'), a composition dealing with the proper Hebrew spelling of Yiddish and Germanic names; the subject of such writing was legal in nature and used for divorce documents, with the intention being that only a normalized and regular spelling will be legally binding to the subjects (and witnesses) in Bet-Din. After this excursus is another called Ḳetsat shemot 'ayyarot ve-neharot, transl. 'various names of cities and rivers', authored by Eliezer b. Solomon Lipshutz for his Damesek Eli'ezer (printed in Neuwied, 1749); this served as a loose-ordered lexicon of Hebraicized names for German-Yiddish areas legalized for Beth-din divorce documents.  General Note  Haskamot on this edition: Samuel Heilman of Metz; Yitshak-Zekyl Ötthausen of Pfersee; Eli'ezer b. Mosheh Katzenellenbogen of Haguenau; Naftali-Hirsch b. Mosheh Katzenellenbogen of Main-Tauber-Kreis with Aube; Nathaniel Weill of Kahrlsruhe; and an introduction of the printer, Haim b. Tsevi-Hirsch of Fürth.",
        "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:   notes    End leaves contain the Seder Giṭin ve-Ḥalitsah, transl. ’Order of Divorce [Laws] and annulment of the Biblical Levirate Marriage’; this is a tract written during the 16th century by Mikhl b. Joseph Reb-Jośpehś Teimer of Krakow, a Halakhist of the 16th century.      Title within ornamental border. This edition is very similar to earlier editions printed by Rofe / Kassman in Amsterdam; this imprint only lists Rofe as the printer.      Leaves numbered in Hebrew.      ‫ דף קעח, ב־קפ״ח: סדר גיטין מהרב מהור״ר מיכל ר׳ יוזפש מק״ק קראקא אשר כבר נדפס בספר חלקת מחוקק. ‬" ;
    dc:publisher "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/dcd40899-377a-56f4-a677-e03e8fc341c2",
        "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/f5b6a429-a026-5d58-9f95-3c7633f8bed2",
        "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/f9ae50fd-0c12-5a29-a35f-0632094d076e" ;
    dc:title "Shulhan Arukh. Even ha-Ezer" ;
    dcterms:created "2024-03-18T00:22:07.314742"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:prefLabel "Shulhan Arukh. Even ha-Ezer" .

