<http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/9a3975d6-683d-5ec8-8369-2845ff553610> a gndo:Work ;
    gndo:associatedPlace "Thessaloniki" ;
    gndo:author "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/109675a8-f653-55a6-9740-705740a627a4" ;
    gndo:dateOfPublication "1752 - 1756" ;
    gndo:formerOwner "Baltimore Hebrew College",
        "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library" ;
    gndo:language "Hebrew" ;
    gndo:owner "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library" ;
    gndo:printer "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/b6f918fc-8ad7-50ca-8b0f-c0b84f9cfb66" ;
    jl:describedAt "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000120957&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0" ;
    jl:hasAbstract "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:  notes    This is a work chiefly dealing with Halakhic reasoning on the order of Maimonides in the Mishneh Torah; it sources both the medieval and early-modern Halakhists as part of a discourse. For example, a longer and evolving discourse often ends with latter-day Poskim (i.e. Halakhic decisors) such as Shabbetai ha-kohen or David ha-levi, both 16th and 17th century Halakhists.      The author was a rabbinical figure in Thessaloniki during the first half of the eighteenth century. He wrote a number of works, some Halakhic and some homiletic and sermonic.   references    Benjacob, I. Otsar ha-sefarim, p. 133, #32      BM Hebrew, p. 47      Friedberg, B. Bet eked sefarim, v.1, p. 289, #727      Encycl. Judaica, v.2, p. 792      Jewish Encycl., v.1, p. 485" ;
    dc:title "Halakhah le-Mosheh" ;
    dcterms:created "2024-03-17T15:17:51.777374"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:prefLabel "Halakhah le-Mosheh" .

