<http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/bfc4e45e-66d4-5998-9bce-573e90e0ea47> a gndo:Work ;
    gndo:associatedPlace "Amsterdam",
        "אמשטרדם" ;
    gndo:author "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/15fc83cf-c486-5394-b15a-3f644edd9648" ;
    gndo:dateOfPublication "1762" ;
    gndo:formerOwner "Baltimore Hebrew College",
        "Dropsie College Library",
        "Isaac Leeser",
        "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library",
        "Tsevi-Yehudah (Runkel)" ;
    gndo:language "Hebrew" ;
    gndo:owner "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library" ;
    gndo:printer "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/329c5cd3-b9e8-55e3-8361-024e5df60c9d" ;
    jl:describedAt "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000169313&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0" ;
    jl:hasAbstract "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:  notes    This volume is a collection of passages elucidating Rashi’s commentary to the Babylonian Talmud Tractate Berakhot; the method used by the author is to seize on the use of the word ke-lomar (כלומר), meaning ’as if to say’, by Rashi. The style used through the passages is highly rhetorical and flowery, with some elements even written in prose; the style is long-hand and reminiscent of oratory-style writing.      Title features two printer’s devices; the header of the title features lions guarding a pendant with an open folio-volume inside. The symbol above the imprint features a roaring lion with the printer’s surname (Sussmans) in it’s center. The printer was named Leib, or Lion.      The author was a rabbinical figure in Altona, one of the Jewish quarters in the city of Hamburg, Germany. He hailed originally from Halberstadt in the Saxony-Anhalt section of Germany.      Haskamot: Saul Lowenstamm of Amsterdam Ashkenazic Community, imploring others to print the volume for the profit of the author, who is impoverished though wants to live honorably (i.e. without charity), and absolving any copyright if a printer uses good intent, dated 1 Elul 1762; Solomon Salem of the Amsterdam Spanish-Portugese Community, 1762; from two rabbinical judges in Berlin, Joel b. Jekuthiel of Glogau and Jeremiah b. Naftali-Hirsch of Halberstadt, dated 9 Kislev 1761; Eli’ezer Lipshutz of Deutz - Hadamar - Runkel, 4 Av 1763; Yehudah-Loeb ha-levi of Grätz (i.e. Grodzisk Wielkopolski, Poland), residing in the home of the wealthy Dani’el (Itzig) in Berlin, 9 Kislev 1762; Nahman ha-levi of Leeuwarden in the Netherlands, 10 Sivan 1763; Eizek ha-Kohen of Hamburg, rabbinical judge of Groningen in the Friesland (northern Netherlands), June 1763. With a lengthy introduction by the author.   references    Steinschneider, M. Cat. Libr. Hebr. Bodl., #5689 : 1.      Friedberg, C.D., Beit Eked Sefarim, mem 2332.      Zedner, J., Cat. Hebrew Books British Library, p. 733." ;
    dc:title "Minḥat Yehuda" ;
    dcterms:created "2024-03-17T18:45:42.143277"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:prefLabel "Minḥat Yehuda" .

