        "Library of the Hungarian Academy of Sciences",
        "New York Public Library" ;
    gndo:printer "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/cd6f7305-d590-5978-96af-8d7f1f4c33ee",
        "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/fd7737ba-3645-571b-8f26-150abbdaf86f" ;
    jl:describedAt "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000105908&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0",
        "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000105909&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0" ;
    jl:hasAbstract "",
        "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:  notes    The translation of Ibn Gabirol’s work is by Judah ben Solomon Ibn Tibbon.      The translation of Sefer ha-tapuaḥ (De pomo) from Arabic to Hebrew is by Abraham ben Samuel bar Hasdai. Aristotle is the attributed author and not the certain author.      The translation of Musar ha-Filosofiah from Arabic into Hebrew is by Judah ben Solomon al-Harizi.      The margins and folios in this edition are as those made for a deluxe library volume.      The title features a miniature specimen of the Counter - Seal of Napolean I (Bonaparte). This seal featured an eagle, and seems to have been employed over the Great Seal of Napolean, which featured a graven image of Bonaparte.      Edition was published by Abraham Parrisac (אברהם פריזעק), who is commended by a certain Liebermann temporarily stationed in Lunéville and originally from Worms. Another added printer / typesetter is Leb b. Eliyahu Hadar, originally from Metz and stationed in Lunéville.      Two forwards are printed preceding the text; these were printed for the first edition printed at Riva de Trento in 1562. The first is by Jacob Marcuria and the second is by Yosef b. Ya’akov Schalit Aschkenazi of Padua.   references    Friedberg, Beit Eked Sefarim, gimel 212 : 2 (second count)" ;
    dc:publisher "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/13407d37-dcef-55b8-9dfe-a16f0c3277e8" ;
