<http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/eec5f11c-f769-5039-b0b8-d6494c55a593> a gndo:Work ;
    gndo:associatedPlace "Frankfurta which is on a desert",
        "פרנקפורטיאה אשר על אודרה" ;
    gndo:author "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/fd9a922a-ecd7-54c7-a063-b590f3c83e78" ;
    gndo:dateOfPublication "1770" ;
    gndo:formerOwner "Avraham A. Price",
        "Baltimore Hebrew College",
        "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library",
        "Yehudah Leib [b.i. Feisel?] Elkish",
        "kloyz ha-hadash Vilna" ;
    gndo:language "Hebrew" ;
    gndo:owner "Joseph Meyerhoff Collection, Towson University Library" ;
    gndo:printer "http://data.judaicalink.org/data/footprints/efd3e3f3-3e87-520a-8002-d5c6a651d939" ;
    jl:describedAt "https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000171365&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=en_US&offset=0" ;
    jl:hasAbstract "All notes by Chaim Meiselman:  notes    The text consists of the Talmudic novellae of Joshua Spira, writing on the tractate of Pesahim. At the end, there is a selection of Responsa from the author on the laws of Passover; most are very lengthy.      The major elements of the title statement is printed in heavy bold type.      The author was the Av-Beth-Din, or Rabbinical Judge, in Zabłudów in the Białystok County in Podlaskie in northeastern Poland; he went on to become the Av-Beth-Din of greater Białystok; after which he moved to Germany and served the rabbinate in Schwerin and the greater Mecklenberg provinces.      Haskamot of Saul Lowenstamm in Amsterdam, 9 Tamuz 1762; ibid, signing in Frankfurt a.d. Oder, 21 Heshvan 1770; Aharon b. Mosheh of Berlin, signing 13 Sivan 1769; David Tevele Schiff, signing in Altona-Hamburg-Wandsbek (although he actually only presided over Hamburg at the time), 1 Heshvan 1768; Israel Lipshutz of Kleve, signing in late 1770; Shmu’el Zanvyl from Brandburg, serving the rabbinate in Strelitz, 2 Nisan 1769; Efraim-Fishel of Tiktin (Tykocin, Poland), 1770; Binyamin b. Tsevi-Hirsch Biszka of Zamość, presiding over the Grätz rabbinate, signing in Hansaplatz (? possibly a part of Koenigsburg, i.e. Kaliningrad) 4 Tamuz 1770.      Opens with novellae of the author’s brother.      Edited with additions by Baruch ben Moses of Kamieniec Litewski.   references    Steinschneider, M. Cat. Libr. Hebr. Bodl., #6052:1.      Zedner, J. Cat. Hebrew Books British Library, p. 733." ;
    dc:title "Hidushe Halakhot (Spira, Joshua ben Zebi)" ;
    dcterms:created "2024-03-17T15:31:22.125110"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hidushe Halakhot (Spira, Joshua ben Zebi)" .

